// ============================================================
// Temporal Domain (L3) — business-temporal (SCD2) reference data
// ============================================================
// The stereotype is what makes the class temporal. Once it carries
// <<temporal.businesstemporal>>, `all()` no longer means "every row" — it means "the
// version in force at a date", and the date becomes part of the query: all(%2024-06-07),
// all(%latest).
//
// Note there are no from/thru PROPERTIES on the class. The milestoning columns live on
// the table and the engine supplies the predicate; exposing them as properties would let
// a query filter on them directly and quietly bypass the whole mechanism.
###Pure
Class <<temporal.businesstemporal>> counterparty::RatingVersion
{
    counterpartyId: String[1];
    rating: String[0..1];
    agency: String[0..1];
    outlook: String[0..1];
    isInvestmentGrade: Boolean[0..1];
}

// L3b — the bitemporal class. all() now takes TWO dates: the business date and the
// processing date, in that order.
Class <<temporal.bitemporal>> products::InstrumentRating
{
    instrumentId: String[1];
    creditRating: String[0..1];
    source: String[0..1];
}
